Scott's Theorem 7.5 states that if \(\mathcal{D}_0\) and \(\mathcal{D}_1\) are effectively given then so is \((\mathcal{D}_0\to\mathcal{D}_1)\), that \(\mathrm{eval}\) and \(\mathrm{curry}\) are computable whenever the domains involved are, and that the computable elements of the function space are exactly the computable maps; the remark that the proof of 3.7 makes substitution of computable functions of several variables computable is not in this closure. `theorem_75` is the conjunction of `funSpace_isEffectivelyGiven`, `evalMap_isComputable`, `curry_isComputable`, `isComputableElement_funPresentation_iff`, and `Xenum_isComputableElement_iff`. A code denotes a finite list of steps: `funListOf` maps each entry code to the pair `funPair` of a domain neighbourhood with a codomain neighbourhood, and `Xenum` returns the step neighbourhood of the decoded list when `funConsChar` equals 1 and the whole space of maps otherwise (`Xenum_pos`, `Xenum_neg`). Scott keeps only those pairs of sequences that determine a nonempty intersection and bounds the work by \(2\cdot 2^q\) sequential checks; `Xenum` is total, and a code that fails the consistency character denotes the master, while `Basic` decides nonemptiness by a bounded universal over masks below \(2^{\mathrm{code}}\). The criterion is still 3.9(i): `bitSelect` enumerates sublists, and the step set is nonempty exactly when every sublist whose inputs have a common lower neighbourhood has a neighbourhood as the intersection of the selected outputs. Each of those tests is the sequential fold in Scott's hint, `consUpd` intersecting the running index with the next selected neighbourhood and conjoining the consistency flag, with `idxchain` naming the resulting intersection. `funSpace_isEffectivelyGiven` unpacks the deciders of the two presentations and supplies `funPresentation`: consistency of two codes is `consPairChar`, which appends the lists and retests when both are consistent and otherwise returns the consistent side because the other enumeration is the whole space (`consPairChar_spec`, `Xenum_inter_eq`), and intersection-equality is `interEqChar`. The inclusion \(\bigcap_{i<q}[X_{n_i},Y_{m_i}]\subseteq[X_k,Y_\ell]\) is the conditional output intersection: `interYsStp` meets \(Y_{m_i}\) only when \(X_k\subseteq X_{n_i}\), `rel_interYs_funList` records that a map in the step set relates \(X_k\) to that intersection, and `stepFun_funListOf_subset_iff` with `subChar_spec` reduces inclusion of one step neighbourhood in another to one such inclusion for every entry of the target, which is Scott's passage from inclusion in an intersection to inclusion in each term and then to \(\bigcap\{Y_{m_i}\mid X_k\subseteq X_{n_i}\}\subseteq Y_\ell\). Equality of two enumerated neighbourhoods is mutual inclusion when both codes are consistent, and the triviality test `trivialChar` when one side is the whole space (`eqEnumChar_spec`). For \(\mathrm{eval}\), `evalMap_rel_prodNbhd_iff` says the product of a function-space neighbourhood with \(X_k\) is related to \(Y_\ell\) exactly when that neighbourhood is contained in the step \([X_k,Y_\ell]\), and `evalMap_isComputable` reindexes the recursively decidable inclusion of `Xenum` along the one-entry code, so the graph is recursive, as Scott concludes from testing the minimal element of 3.9(ii). For \(\mathrm{curry}\), which the prose leaves as an exercise, `curry_rel_Xenum_iff` states that a domain neighbourhood is related to an enumerated neighbourhood exactly when consistency of the code implies that the original map sends the product of that neighbourhood with each listed input to the corresponding output, and `curry_isComputable` shows this predicate is recursively enumerable as the disjunction of failure of `funConsChar` with a bounded universal of the given graph. The element clause is `mem_Xenum_iff` together with `mem_Xenum_iff_map`: on a consistent code, membership is the universal of \(X_{n_i}\,f\,Y_{m_i}\), and on an inconsistent code it is the master axiom. `Xenum_isComputableElement_iff` is Scott's equivalence: a computable graph makes the index set recursively enumerable by that bounded universal, and a recursively enumerable index set makes the graph recursively enumerable by restricting to one-entry codes, which are consistent because a single step neighbourhood is nonempty. **Theorem:** yes — effective givenness of the function space, computability of \(\mathrm{eval}\) and \(\mathrm{curry}\), and the identification of computable elements with computable maps are the five conjuncts of `theorem_75`. **Proof:** yes — nonemptiness, the conditional-output form of inclusion, decidable evaluation, the currying implication, and the passage between an r.e. graph and an r.e. filter are the arguments above; the search runs up to \(2^{\mathrm{code}}\) rather than Scott's \(2\cdot 2^q\), and a non-determining code denotes the master instead of being left out of the enumeration.
